1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,718
In part one we worked out a single derivative by hand:

2
00:00:04,718 --> 00:00:08,734
the gradient of the surprise with respect to the scores,

3
00:00:08,734 --> 00:00:12,349
the model's guess minus the truth, for natural-log cross-entropy.

4
00:00:12,349 --> 00:00:17,168
In bits there is an extra factor of one over ln two.

5
00:00:17,168 --> 00:00:19,979
The recorded parameter counts are about 124

6
00:00:19,979 --> 00:00:23,594
million for GPT-2 and 4.02 billion for Qwen3-4B.

7
00:00:23,594 --> 00:00:26,004
Each source is labeled on screen.

8
00:00:26,004 --> 00:00:27,209
In full-parameter training,

9
00:00:27,209 --> 00:00:30,422
every step needs the derivative of the loss

10
00:00:30,422 --> 00:00:32,831
with respect to every trainable parameter,

11
00:00:32,831 --> 00:00:37,143
and nobody works those out by hand.

12
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:42,351
The mathematics is three centuries older than the computers that run it.

13
00:00:42,351 --> 00:00:44,811
Leibniz printed the calculus in 1684,

14
00:00:44,811 --> 00:00:47,679
while Newton had his own, his fluxions.

15
00:00:47,679 --> 00:00:51,368
In 1788 Lagrange showed how to optimize under constraints,

16
00:00:51,368 --> 00:00:54,237
and in 1847 Cauchy proposed walking downhill.

17
00:00:54,237 --> 00:00:57,516
Karush, and then Kuhn and Tucker, added inequalities.

18
00:00:57,516 --> 00:00:58,745
Then came programs:

19
00:00:58,745 --> 00:01:03,254
in 1964 Wengert had one carry derivatives along with its values,

20
00:01:03,254 --> 00:01:06,942
and in 1970 Linnainmaa ran the chain rule backwards.

21
00:01:06,942 --> 00:01:10,221
In 1985 a standard fixed how computers round,

22
00:01:10,221 --> 00:01:13,500
and in 1986 Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams demonstrated

23
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:15,549
learning hidden representations with backpropagation.

24
00:01:15,549 --> 00:01:20,057
From 2010, libraries did all of it automatically, and some of

25
00:01:20,057 --> 00:01:25,656
today's largest models train with numbers only eight bits long.

26
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:28,881
This is Part 4 of AI, Built:

27
00:01:28,881 --> 00:01:31,011
change, the calculus of learning.

28
00:01:31,011 --> 00:01:31,863
In it:

29
00:01:31,863 --> 00:01:34,419
derivatives as the best straight-line approximations,

30
00:01:34,419 --> 00:01:36,974
and the derivatives of real layers;

31
00:01:36,974 --> 00:01:40,807
the two ways to run the chain rule, counted;

32
00:01:40,807 --> 00:01:43,789
backpropagation by hand, then our own library,

33
00:01:43,789 --> 00:01:46,344
and a model trained with it;

34
00:01:46,344 --> 00:01:49,752
what goes wrong with numbers in a computer;

35
00:01:49,752 --> 00:01:50,178
memory;

36
00:01:50,178 --> 00:01:52,733
curvature, from Newton's method to the

37
00:01:52,733 --> 00:01:55,289
shape of a trained network's landscape;

38
00:01:55,289 --> 00:01:58,270
and optimizing under constraints, which ends with

39
00:01:58,270 --> 00:02:00,400
differentiating through an equation's solution.

40
00:02:02,367 --> 00:02:03,358
Chapter one.

41
00:02:03,358 --> 00:02:05,834
The derivative.

42
00:02:05,834 --> 00:02:09,522
Zoom in on a smooth curve, closer and closer,

43
00:02:09,522 --> 00:02:13,211
and it starts to look like a straight line.

44
00:02:13,211 --> 00:02:16,490
The slope of that line is the derivative:

45
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:22,908
how much the output changes, for each small change of the input.

46
00:02:23,167 --> 00:02:24,005
Written down:

47
00:02:24,005 --> 00:02:29,454
f at x plus a small step h, is about f of x,

48
00:02:29,454 --> 00:02:31,550
plus the derivative times h.

49
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:34,903
The smaller the step, the better the guess.

50
00:02:34,903 --> 00:02:37,418
A derivative is the best straight-line

51
00:02:37,418 --> 00:02:41,852
description of a function, near one point.

52
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:46,259
The idea is Leibniz's, in print in 1684, and Newton's,

53
00:02:46,259 --> 00:02:51,248
who had worked it out in the 1660s and called it fluxions.

54
00:02:51,248 --> 00:02:53,328
Here is the precise version.

55
00:02:53,328 --> 00:02:57,070
A function is differentiable at x if one number,

56
00:02:57,070 --> 00:03:00,397
the derivative, makes the straight-line guess so good

57
00:03:00,397 --> 00:03:04,139
that its error shrinks faster than the step itself.

58
00:03:04,139 --> 00:03:07,050
Measure it, for Part 1's surprise curve.

59
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:09,961
On these logarithmic axes, the error with

60
00:03:09,961 --> 00:03:13,703
the true slope falls with a slope of 2.00:

61
00:03:13,703 --> 00:03:14,951
like h squared.

62
00:03:14,951 --> 00:03:17,861
Use a slope just ten percent off,

63
00:03:17,861 --> 00:03:22,435
and the error falls only like h, a slope of 1.01.

64
00:03:22,435 --> 00:03:25,762
Exactly one line wins as the step shrinks.

65
00:03:25,762 --> 00:03:29,341
That is what best means.

66
00:03:29,634 --> 00:03:31,696
Let's use the definition once,

67
00:03:31,696 --> 00:03:35,822
on the surprise, log base two of one over p.

68
00:03:35,822 --> 00:03:40,360
The change over a step h is a difference of logarithms,

69
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:43,247
which is the logarithm of a ratio.

70
00:03:43,247 --> 00:03:46,548
Near one, the logarithm is its own tangent:

71
00:03:46,548 --> 00:03:51,911
log of one plus u is u, plus an error smaller than u.

72
00:03:51,911 --> 00:03:57,686
So the number in front of h is minus one over p log two.

73
00:03:57,686 --> 00:04:01,812
At a quarter, that's -5.77 bits per unit of p:

74
00:04:01,812 --> 00:04:05,374
the slope the lens showed.

75
00:04:05,634 --> 00:04:08,899
A few derivatives come up over and over.

76
00:04:08,899 --> 00:04:11,348
The exponential is its own derivative.

77
00:04:11,348 --> 00:04:14,205
The logarithm's derivative is one over x.

78
00:04:14,205 --> 00:04:18,287
That's why the surprise climbs so steeply as p shrinks.

79
00:04:18,287 --> 00:04:21,144
And the chain rule, for a composition:

80
00:04:21,144 --> 00:04:25,634
f of g of x means do g first, then f.

81
00:04:25,634 --> 00:04:30,123
Writing f prime for the slope of f, the derivative of

82
00:04:30,123 --> 00:04:37,062
f of g of x is f prime at g of x, times g prime at x.

83
00:04:37,062 --> 00:04:40,736
Change flows through a composition, multiplying as it goes.

84
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:43,491
Chapter two.

85
00:04:43,491 --> 00:04:45,967
Many inputs.

86
00:04:45,967 --> 00:04:49,337
A loss depends on many numbers at once.

87
00:04:49,337 --> 00:04:52,707
With two, it's a surface over a plane.

88
00:04:52,707 --> 00:04:56,077
Nudge one number while holding the other still,

89
00:04:56,077 --> 00:04:58,604
and you get a partial derivative:

90
00:04:58,604 --> 00:05:00,711
the slope along that axis.

91
00:05:00,711 --> 00:05:06,187
The best flat approximation to a surface near a point is a plane,

92
00:05:06,187 --> 00:05:09,557
the tangent plane, tilted by those two slopes;

93
00:05:09,557 --> 00:05:15,455
its error falls like the square of the distance, just as the line's did:

94
00:05:15,455 --> 00:05:17,561
measured, a slope of 2.00.

95
00:05:17,561 --> 00:05:22,195
Collect the slopes into one vector, and you have the gradient.

96
00:05:22,195 --> 00:05:27,250
It points uphill, and, as we'll prove, the steepest way there is.

97
00:05:27,250 --> 00:05:30,620
So step the other way, again and again,

98
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:35,069
and you slide down into a valley.

99
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:36,718
Why steepest?

100
00:05:36,718 --> 00:05:39,579
At a differentiable point, pick any direction,

101
00:05:39,579 --> 00:05:44,077
a unit vector u, and ask for the slope that way.

102
00:05:44,077 --> 00:05:46,121
The tangent plane answers it:

103
00:05:46,121 --> 00:05:50,209
the slope along u is the gradient dotted with u.

104
00:05:50,209 --> 00:05:54,706
By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality from Part 3, a dot product

105
00:05:54,706 --> 00:06:00,021
is at most the product of the lengths, and u has length one:

106
00:06:00,021 --> 00:06:04,109
no direction climbs faster than the length of the gradient.

107
00:06:04,109 --> 00:06:06,154
If the gradient is nonzero,

108
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:09,015
the maximum occurs in its positive direction.

109
00:06:09,015 --> 00:06:13,104
So the gradient's own direction is the steepest way up,

110
00:06:13,104 --> 00:06:15,557
its opposite the steepest way down,

111
00:06:15,557 --> 00:06:19,645
and the directions at right angles give zero first-order change.

112
00:06:19,645 --> 00:06:25,233
If the gradient is zero, every directional derivative is zero.

113
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:28,429
Seen from above, the surface becomes a

114
00:06:28,429 --> 00:06:31,777
map of contour lines, each at one height.

115
00:06:31,777 --> 00:06:35,961
Swing a direction around the point and read the slope.

116
00:06:35,961 --> 00:06:38,472
It's largest straight along the gradient,

117
00:06:38,472 --> 00:06:41,819
zero along the tangent to the contour line,

118
00:06:41,819 --> 00:06:44,330
and most negative pointing against it.

119
00:06:44,330 --> 00:06:48,515
Measured by finite differences in seven hundred and twenty directions,

120
00:06:48,515 --> 00:06:52,699
the steepest slope is 0.8126, the gradient's length is 0.8126,

121
00:06:52,699 --> 00:06:56,047
and the two directions are 0.21 degrees apart:

122
00:06:56,047 --> 00:06:59,639
exactly as the inequality says.

123
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:04,367
Walking downhill this way is the oldest method in this series.

124
00:07:04,367 --> 00:07:05,991
In 1847 Augustin-Louis Cauchy,

125
00:07:05,991 --> 00:07:09,240
wanting to solve the equations of an orbit,

126
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:12,489
wrote them as a sum of squares and

127
00:07:12,489 --> 00:07:14,519
proposed stepping against its gradient.

128
00:07:14,519 --> 00:07:15,331
Written today:

129
00:07:15,331 --> 00:07:18,174
the next point is the current one,

130
00:07:18,174 --> 00:07:21,829
minus a step size eta times the gradient there.

131
00:07:21,829 --> 00:07:27,108
How large a step, and how fast it arrives, depends on the curvature,

132
00:07:27,108 --> 00:07:31,169
which comes in chapter fourteen and again in Part 11.

133
00:07:32,966 --> 00:07:33,907
Chapter three.

134
00:07:33,907 --> 00:07:36,818
Maps that bend.

135
00:07:36,833 --> 00:07:41,373
Now let a function take a whole vector to another vector,

136
00:07:41,373 --> 00:07:44,675
the way a layer of a network does.

137
00:07:44,675 --> 00:07:47,151
Here's one that bends the plane:

138
00:07:47,151 --> 00:07:48,802
the grid lines curve.

139
00:07:48,802 --> 00:07:53,755
But zoom in close to any point, and the curves straighten out.

140
00:07:53,755 --> 00:07:57,470
Up close, every smooth map looks like a matrix:

141
00:07:57,470 --> 00:08:02,685
its Jacobian, the table of all its partial derivatives.

142
00:08:02,966 --> 00:08:09,463
In general, a map from n numbers to m numbers has an m by n Jacobian:

143
00:08:09,463 --> 00:08:12,712
one row per output, one column per input,

144
00:08:12,712 --> 00:08:16,367
the one matrix that makes the straight-line guess best.

145
00:08:16,367 --> 00:08:20,428
A gradient is the special case of a single output.

146
00:08:20,428 --> 00:08:22,052
For this two-dimensional map,

147
00:08:22,052 --> 00:08:24,895
the absolute value of the Jacobian's determinant,

148
00:08:24,895 --> 00:08:28,143
from part three, gives the local area factor.

149
00:08:28,143 --> 00:08:31,798
And the chain rule for maps is matrix multiplication:

150
00:08:31,798 --> 00:08:35,859
the Jacobian of one map after another is the product

151
00:08:35,859 --> 00:08:39,514
of their Jacobians, each taken where its input lands.

152
00:08:39,514 --> 00:08:42,762
Applying this chain rule backwards is the basis

153
00:08:42,762 --> 00:08:47,105
of backpropagation, as chapter five will show.

154
00:08:47,366 --> 00:08:50,250
Chain many maps and their Jacobians multiply.

155
00:08:50,250 --> 00:08:55,195
The product's norm is at most the product of the individual norms;

156
00:08:55,195 --> 00:08:57,255
it need not equal it.

157
00:08:57,255 --> 00:09:01,787
Here is a deep stack of tanh layers, 60 of them,

158
00:09:01,787 --> 00:09:04,672
with random weights scaled by a gain.

159
00:09:04,672 --> 00:09:08,380
We plot the mean log of the product's largest

160
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:11,264
singular value, with the range across seeds.

161
00:09:11,264 --> 00:09:13,736
This is the maximum possible amplification,

162
00:09:13,736 --> 00:09:16,621
also for the transposed product in backpropagation,

163
00:09:16,621 --> 00:09:19,093
not what happens in every direction.

164
00:09:19,093 --> 00:09:21,977
A norm below one shrinks every direction;

165
00:09:21,977 --> 00:09:25,685
a norm above one allows growth in some direction.

166
00:09:25,685 --> 00:09:29,806
At gain 0.5, the mean log size ends at -17.4.

167
00:09:29,806 --> 00:09:32,690
At gain 2, it ends at 4.6.

168
00:09:32,690 --> 00:09:38,871
At gain one, its mean log size stays within one power of ten of zero.

169
00:09:38,871 --> 00:09:42,167
Such products help explain vanishing and exploding gradients.

170
00:09:42,167 --> 00:09:46,699
Parts 9 and 10 return to how initialization affects their scale.

171
00:09:48,433 --> 00:09:49,317
Chapter four.

172
00:09:49,317 --> 00:09:53,026
The Jacobians of real layers.

173
00:09:53,033 --> 00:09:54,273
Real layers first.

174
00:09:54,273 --> 00:09:57,580
The most common one is a matrix product:

175
00:09:57,580 --> 00:10:01,301
inputs X, one row per token, times weights W.

176
00:10:01,301 --> 00:10:07,502
A weight in row j and column k touches only column k of the output,

177
00:10:07,502 --> 00:10:11,223
so its gradient collects the gradient arriving from above,

178
00:10:11,223 --> 00:10:13,703
G, times the inputs it multiplied.

179
00:10:13,703 --> 00:10:16,597
That sum is itself a matrix product:

180
00:10:16,597 --> 00:10:18,251
X transposed times G.

181
00:10:18,251 --> 00:10:21,558
And the gradient for the inputs, which flows

182
00:10:21,558 --> 00:10:25,692
on to the layer below, is G times W transposed.

183
00:10:25,692 --> 00:10:28,172
Notice what we never wrote down:

184
00:10:28,172 --> 00:10:29,413
the Jacobian itself.

185
00:10:29,413 --> 00:10:32,306
For one of GPT-2's matrices over 1,024

186
00:10:32,306 --> 00:10:35,200
tokens it would hold 7.4 trillion numbers.

187
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:38,094
Backpropagation needs only its product with G,

188
00:10:38,094 --> 00:10:44,968
which costs about as much as the layer did on the way forward.

189
00:10:45,233 --> 00:10:48,515
Next, the softmax, which turns scores into probabilities.

190
00:10:48,515 --> 00:10:50,567
Differentiate with the quotient rule.

191
00:10:50,567 --> 00:10:53,849
Split the fraction, and every entry is p

192
00:10:53,849 --> 00:10:58,363
i times delta i j, minus p i times p j.

193
00:10:58,363 --> 00:10:59,594
As a matrix:

194
00:10:59,594 --> 00:11:01,645
the probabilities on the diagonal,

195
00:11:01,645 --> 00:11:04,927
minus the outer product of p with itself.

196
00:11:04,927 --> 00:11:09,441
Here it is for five scores, computed, and matching our library.

197
00:11:09,441 --> 00:11:12,723
Its rows add to zero, because adding the

198
00:11:12,723 --> 00:11:15,595
same number to every score changes nothing;

199
00:11:15,595 --> 00:11:20,519
and it is symmetric with no negative eigenvalues, one of them zero.

200
00:11:20,519 --> 00:11:22,981
Again we never need the matrix:

201
00:11:22,981 --> 00:11:27,904
its product with a vector takes n steps instead of n squared,

202
00:11:27,904 --> 00:11:32,007
which matters with GPT-2's fifty thousand tokens in the vocabulary.

203
00:11:32,007 --> 00:11:35,290
Joined with the cross-entropy, it collapses to Part

204
00:11:35,290 --> 00:11:39,393
1's q minus p when the loss uses natural logarithms;

205
00:11:39,393 --> 00:11:45,816
a loss in bits adds a factor of one over ln two.

206
00:11:46,100 --> 00:11:47,349
Last, layer norm:

207
00:11:47,349 --> 00:11:51,512
subtract the mean of the vector, divide by its spread,

208
00:11:51,512 --> 00:11:54,010
before any learned scale and shift.

209
00:11:54,010 --> 00:11:57,757
The mean and the spread move when x moves,

210
00:11:57,757 --> 00:12:00,256
so the Jacobian has two corrections:

211
00:12:00,256 --> 00:12:04,003
a projection that removes the direction of all ones,

212
00:12:04,003 --> 00:12:08,582
and another that suppresses the radial direction, all divided by sigma.

213
00:12:08,582 --> 00:12:11,913
Computed for six numbers, 2 eigenvalues have magnitude

214
00:12:11,913 --> 00:12:15,244
below one thousandth, and the rest are equal.

215
00:12:15,244 --> 00:12:17,326
Mean shifts are removed exactly;

216
00:12:17,326 --> 00:12:19,407
scale changes are strongly suppressed,

217
00:12:19,407 --> 00:12:22,322
with a small remaining sensitivity from epsilon.

218
00:12:24,099 --> 00:12:25,140
Chapter five.

219
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:28,201
Forwards, or backwards?

220
00:12:28,232 --> 00:12:33,622
There are two ways to use a Jacobian without ever writing it down.

221
00:12:33,622 --> 00:12:35,281
Push a direction forward:

222
00:12:35,281 --> 00:12:38,597
if the inputs move by v, the outputs

223
00:12:38,597 --> 00:12:41,500
move by J v, a Jacobian-vector product.

224
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,573
Or pull a sensitivity back:

225
00:12:43,573 --> 00:12:46,475
if the outputs matter with weights u,

226
00:12:46,475 --> 00:12:50,621
the inputs matter by u transposed J, a vector-Jacobian product.

227
00:12:50,621 --> 00:12:55,181
A network is a chain of maps, from its parameters, theta,

228
00:12:55,181 --> 00:13:00,571
to the loss, and its Jacobian is a product of the links' Jacobians.

229
00:13:00,571 --> 00:13:03,888
A JVP multiplies that product from the right,

230
00:13:03,888 --> 00:13:06,376
in the order the network runs:

231
00:13:06,376 --> 00:13:07,205
forward mode.

232
00:13:07,205 --> 00:13:11,351
A VJP multiplies from the left, starting at the loss:

233
00:13:11,351 --> 00:13:12,180
reverse mode.

234
00:13:12,180 --> 00:13:15,911
Either one is about one pass through the network.

235
00:13:15,911 --> 00:13:23,631
For the whole Jacobian you need a JVP per input, or a VJP per output.

236
00:13:23,899 --> 00:13:24,714
Let's count.

237
00:13:24,714 --> 00:13:28,381
These are networks with two hidden layers of sixty-four,

238
00:13:28,381 --> 00:13:33,677
with n inputs and m outputs, from one to two hundred and fifty-six.

239
00:13:33,677 --> 00:13:36,936
We count dense derivative matrix products and tanh

240
00:13:36,936 --> 00:13:39,788
multiplications, relative to a reference primal pass;

241
00:13:39,788 --> 00:13:42,232
this is not every scalar operation.

242
00:13:42,232 --> 00:13:45,084
The full Jacobian takes n forward directions.

243
00:13:45,084 --> 00:13:46,714
Or m reverse directions.

244
00:13:46,714 --> 00:13:49,973
Few inputs and many outputs favor forward mode;

245
00:13:49,973 --> 00:13:53,232
many inputs and few outputs favor reverse mode.

246
00:13:53,232 --> 00:13:56,084
Timings include primal computation and implementation overhead,

247
00:13:56,084 --> 00:13:58,121
separately from the derivative count.

248
00:13:58,121 --> 00:14:02,603
In this recorded benchmark, across the 20 networks where the counts

249
00:14:02,603 --> 00:14:09,399
differ, the cheaper mode by count was also the faster in every one.

250
00:14:09,699 --> 00:14:11,770
Training is the extreme case:

251
00:14:11,770 --> 00:14:16,740
every parameter is an input, and the loss is a single output.

252
00:14:16,740 --> 00:14:21,297
Forwards, you carry a matrix with a column for every parameter.

253
00:14:21,297 --> 00:14:25,853
Backwards, a single row, the size of the layer you've reached.

254
00:14:25,853 --> 00:14:28,338
Our small classifier has 874 parameters.

255
00:14:28,338 --> 00:14:31,238
Forward mode pushes 874 directions through it:

256
00:14:31,238 --> 00:14:35,794
counted, 874 times the dense derivative work of one backward pass,

257
00:14:35,794 --> 00:14:38,279
counting matrix products and tanh multiplications.

258
00:14:38,279 --> 00:14:42,007
Only parameter gradients are requested, not an input gradient.

259
00:14:42,007 --> 00:14:44,906
The observed forward-to-reverse call-time ratio is 64.81.

260
00:14:44,906 --> 00:14:47,391
These means include different implementation and

261
00:14:47,391 --> 00:14:49,877
graph-building costs, with no confidence interval;

262
00:14:49,877 --> 00:14:53,190
the work count is not a universal lower

263
00:14:53,190 --> 00:14:55,261
bound for optimized forward differentiation.

264
00:14:55,261 --> 00:14:57,746
The measured gradient difference is shown.

265
00:14:57,746 --> 00:15:00,646
For GPT-2 the same column-by-column strategy would

266
00:15:00,646 --> 00:15:03,545
require 124,439,808 directions against one reverse pass.

267
00:15:03,545 --> 00:15:06,859
That is why standard large-parameter gradient training usually

268
00:15:06,859 --> 00:15:10,172
uses reverse mode, called backpropagation for neural networks.

269
00:15:10,172 --> 00:15:14,986
Forward and hybrid methods still have other uses.

270
00:15:15,232 --> 00:15:18,060
Reverse mode was found more than once.

271
00:15:18,060 --> 00:15:22,503
Seppo Linnainmaa described it in his 1970 master's thesis in Helsinki,

272
00:15:22,503 --> 00:15:26,542
to work out how rounding errors accumulate through a program.

273
00:15:26,542 --> 00:15:30,985
Paul Werbos proposed it for training networks in his 1974 thesis,

274
00:15:30,985 --> 00:15:34,216
and in 1986 David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and

275
00:15:34,216 --> 00:15:37,851
Ronald Williams showed, in Nature, what it could do:

276
00:15:37,851 --> 00:15:40,678
networks that learn their own internal representations.

277
00:15:40,678 --> 00:15:44,314
Control engineers had already used the same backward recursion

278
00:15:44,314 --> 00:15:48,353
around 1960, to steer rockets along the best flight paths.

279
00:15:50,099 --> 00:15:51,140
Chapter six.

280
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:54,201
Backpropagation, by hand.

281
00:15:54,232 --> 00:16:00,293
Let's run it by hand, on the smallest network there is, drawn as a

282
00:16:00,293 --> 00:16:05,054
flowchart of operations called a computation graph, each circle a node.

283
00:16:05,054 --> 00:16:09,816
One input, x, times one weight, plus one bias, a shift;

284
00:16:09,816 --> 00:16:12,413
then a squashing function, the sigmoid,

285
00:16:12,413 --> 00:16:17,607
an S-curve that presses any number into the range zero to one;

286
00:16:17,607 --> 00:16:19,339
and a squared error:

287
00:16:19,339 --> 00:16:22,369
the miss from the target y, squared.

288
00:16:22,369 --> 00:16:23,235
Forwards first:

289
00:16:23,235 --> 00:16:29,366
the values flow from left to right, and end in the loss, 0.854.

290
00:16:29,366 --> 00:16:30,195
Now backwards.

291
00:16:30,195 --> 00:16:35,169
Start at the loss, with a gradient of one, and walk back.

292
00:16:35,169 --> 00:16:39,313
Each step multiplies the gradient arriving from the right by

293
00:16:39,313 --> 00:16:43,873
its own local derivative, and passes the result to the left.

294
00:16:43,873 --> 00:16:47,189
At the weight, the gradient is minus 0.259:

295
00:16:47,189 --> 00:16:49,261
raise the weight a little,

296
00:16:49,261 --> 00:16:53,406
and the loss falls by about a quarter as much.

297
00:16:53,406 --> 00:16:59,051
No step needed to know anything but its own derivative.

298
00:16:59,299 --> 00:17:00,523
One more rule.

299
00:17:00,523 --> 00:17:03,378
When a value feeds into two places,

300
00:17:03,378 --> 00:17:07,049
the gradients coming back from both are added together.

301
00:17:07,049 --> 00:17:08,680
That's the whole algorithm:

302
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:11,127
visit the nodes in reverse order,

303
00:17:11,127 --> 00:17:17,114
and let each one pass its gradient back to its inputs.

304
00:17:17,366 --> 00:17:19,843
Now a real network, in matrices:

305
00:17:19,843 --> 00:17:24,798
an input x, a layer with weights W one and a tanh,

306
00:17:24,798 --> 00:17:28,102
a second layer, a softmax and the cross-entropy.

307
00:17:28,102 --> 00:17:31,405
Call the gradient with respect to a layer's

308
00:17:31,405 --> 00:17:34,708
pre-activation z its delta, the error signal there.

309
00:17:34,708 --> 00:17:38,425
At the top, delta two is Part 1's answer:

310
00:17:38,425 --> 00:17:41,315
the predicted probabilities minus the one-hot truth.

311
00:17:41,315 --> 00:17:45,857
Each weight in W two multiplies one input into one output,

312
00:17:45,857 --> 00:17:49,987
so its gradient is that output's delta times that input:

313
00:17:49,987 --> 00:17:53,703
an outer product, delta two times a one transposed.

314
00:17:53,703 --> 00:17:58,245
To go down a layer, pull delta back through W two,

315
00:17:58,245 --> 00:18:01,961
which for a matrix means multiplying by its transpose,

316
00:18:01,961 --> 00:18:05,678
and then through the tanh, multiplying by its slope.

317
00:18:05,678 --> 00:18:07,742
And the same rule again:

318
00:18:07,742 --> 00:18:12,285
the gradient of W one is delta one times x transposed.

319
00:18:12,285 --> 00:18:17,088
Those four lines are backpropagation, for any depth.

320
00:18:17,366 --> 00:18:21,034
Here is that outer product for one example, computed:

321
00:18:21,034 --> 00:18:26,740
the error signal at the hidden layer, a column, times the input, a row.

322
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:31,630
Every row of the result is a multiple of the same input,

323
00:18:31,630 --> 00:18:34,891
so the matrix has rank at most one.

324
00:18:34,891 --> 00:18:37,336
Here its measured rank is 1:

325
00:18:37,336 --> 00:18:40,189
one nonzero singular direction, up to rounding.

326
00:18:40,189 --> 00:18:44,672
A batch adds 8 such matrices, so its rank is at

327
00:18:44,672 --> 00:18:47,933
most the batch size and the matrix dimensions.

328
00:18:47,933 --> 00:18:52,008
Here it reaches rank 4, the maximum for this shape.

329
00:18:52,008 --> 00:18:54,861
That structure comes back when models are

330
00:18:54,861 --> 00:18:57,714
fine-tuned with low-rank adapters in Part 27.

331
00:18:57,714 --> 00:18:59,752
And these hand-derived gradients match

332
00:18:59,752 --> 00:19:02,605
our library and PyTorch with the recorded

333
00:19:02,605 --> 00:19:04,643
maximum absolute differences shown here,

334
00:19:04,643 --> 00:19:06,681
including zero if they coincide.

335
00:19:08,431 --> 00:19:09,422
Chapter seven.

336
00:19:09,422 --> 00:19:12,898
The machine, in code.

337
00:19:12,898 --> 00:19:14,955
Here it is in code:

338
00:19:14,955 --> 00:19:19,069
the series' own automatic differentiation, about 200 lines in all.

339
00:19:19,069 --> 00:19:22,361
Every operation remembers its inputs, along with a

340
00:19:22,361 --> 00:19:26,475
small function that sends a gradient back to each one.

341
00:19:26,475 --> 00:19:27,298
Here's multiplication:

342
00:19:27,298 --> 00:19:30,177
the gradient for one input is the

343
00:19:30,177 --> 00:19:32,646
incoming gradient, times the other input.

344
00:19:32,646 --> 00:19:35,937
And backward visits the graph in reverse order,

345
00:19:35,937 --> 00:19:41,963
adds up what arrives at each node, and passes it on.

346
00:19:42,231 --> 00:19:43,883
Backward needs an order:

347
00:19:43,883 --> 00:19:50,074
a node may pass its gradient on only after every use of it has reported.

348
00:19:50,074 --> 00:19:53,789
A depth-first search through the graph gives exactly that,

349
00:19:53,789 --> 00:19:57,092
a topological order, each node after its inputs.

350
00:19:57,092 --> 00:20:02,045
Here is chapter six's example, numbered by our library in that order.

351
00:20:02,045 --> 00:20:04,934
Written out, the graph is a list,

352
00:20:04,934 --> 00:20:08,237
each line one elementary operation on earlier lines.

353
00:20:08,237 --> 00:20:09,475
In 1964 R.

354
00:20:09,475 --> 00:20:09,888
E.

355
00:20:09,888 --> 00:20:12,777
Wengert had programs keep such lists to

356
00:20:12,777 --> 00:20:15,254
carry derivatives along with their values;

357
00:20:15,254 --> 00:20:18,143
tools today call the record a tape.

358
00:20:18,143 --> 00:20:21,033
Forwards, the values run down the list.

359
00:20:21,033 --> 00:20:24,335
Backwards, the list runs from the bottom up,

360
00:20:24,335 --> 00:20:29,963
each line sending its gradient to the lines it used.

361
00:20:30,231 --> 00:20:31,488
Does it work?

362
00:20:31,488 --> 00:20:35,258
Nudge each checked number a millionth in both directions,

363
00:20:35,258 --> 00:20:37,771
take the central difference, and compare:

364
00:20:37,771 --> 00:20:42,379
the largest absolute difference is 2.20 times ten to the -10.

365
00:20:42,379 --> 00:20:46,149
Chapter ten explains the limits of that numerical check.

366
00:20:46,149 --> 00:20:48,243
Against PyTorch, an independent automatic

367
00:20:48,243 --> 00:20:49,919
differentiation implementation, the largest

368
00:20:49,919 --> 00:20:53,689
absolute difference is 2.78 times ten to the -17.

369
00:20:53,689 --> 00:20:59,796
These are absolute errors, not a count of matching printed digits.

370
00:21:00,031 --> 00:21:03,801
Ours is the smallest member of a long family.

371
00:21:03,801 --> 00:21:07,989
In 1980 Bert Speelpenning compiled reverse mode from ordinary programs.

372
00:21:07,989 --> 00:21:11,759
In 2010 Theano, from Montreal, had you declare a

373
00:21:11,759 --> 00:21:15,110
graph first and then compiled it, gradients included.

374
00:21:15,110 --> 00:21:19,298
In 2015 Chainer recorded the graph as the code ran,

375
00:21:19,298 --> 00:21:23,068
define by run, while TensorFlow kept a static graph.

376
00:21:23,068 --> 00:21:29,350
PyTorch, in 2017, ran define by run on GPUs, as ours does on the CPU,

377
00:21:29,350 --> 00:21:33,539
and in 2018 JAX traced whole functions and transformed them:

378
00:21:33,539 --> 00:21:36,470
gradients, both kinds of product, compilation, batching.

379
00:21:36,470 --> 00:21:41,078
The line you write is almost the same in all three.

380
00:21:42,831 --> 00:21:43,738
Chapter eight.

381
00:21:43,738 --> 00:21:47,031
Training with our library.

382
00:21:47,031 --> 00:21:50,354
Before we train, look at the first gradient.

383
00:21:50,354 --> 00:21:52,431
Differentiate the average natural-log surprise,

384
00:21:52,431 --> 00:21:56,585
in nats, with respect to one score, z i j:

385
00:21:56,585 --> 00:22:02,401
every pair of letters that starts with i adds its guess minus its truth.

386
00:22:02,401 --> 00:22:06,140
Collected, that's the share of pairs starting with i,

387
00:22:06,140 --> 00:22:09,048
times Part 1's q minus p hat:

388
00:22:09,048 --> 00:22:11,956
the model's probabilities minus the counted ones.

389
00:22:11,956 --> 00:22:15,279
At the start every guess is one twenty-seventh.

390
00:22:15,279 --> 00:22:19,433
Computed on the books, the library's gradient matches the formula,

391
00:22:19,433 --> 00:22:24,003
and the biggest pushes, in red, go to the commonest pairs.

392
00:22:24,003 --> 00:22:28,157
Gradient descent is counting, done a little at a time.

393
00:22:28,157 --> 00:22:34,642
The plots report bits by dividing the natural-log loss by log two.

394
00:22:34,897 --> 00:22:36,136
Now let's train.

395
00:22:36,136 --> 00:22:39,852
Counting gives a reference of 3.331 bits per letter.

396
00:22:39,852 --> 00:22:43,982
The training loop instead samples letter pairs from the books.

397
00:22:43,982 --> 00:22:47,698
It learns 729 raw scores, initially zero, with Adam.

398
00:22:47,698 --> 00:22:49,763
The picture shows their row-normalized

399
00:22:49,763 --> 00:22:52,653
natural log probabilities, not the raw scores.

400
00:22:52,653 --> 00:22:55,956
Its scale is shared with the counted comparison:

401
00:22:55,956 --> 00:22:58,847
values below minus eight are equally dark.

402
00:22:58,847 --> 00:23:02,976
Underneath, samples use the same random dice at every checkpoint.

403
00:23:02,976 --> 00:23:05,454
Heatmaps are after the labeled update;

404
00:23:05,454 --> 00:23:09,170
the curve records minibatch loss just before that update.

405
00:23:09,170 --> 00:23:14,231
The final train and new-text numbers use the full respective corpora.

406
00:23:14,231 --> 00:23:18,389
After 1500 Adam updates, the full-training loss is 3.335 bits

407
00:23:18,389 --> 00:23:22,547
per letter, within 0.02 bits of the counting reference 3.331.

408
00:23:22,547 --> 00:23:24,626
This is a finite-run result.

409
00:23:24,626 --> 00:23:27,953
On 552 letter pairs whose counted conditional probability

410
00:23:27,953 --> 00:23:30,864
exceeds 0.0001, the normalized log probabilities have

411
00:23:30,864 --> 00:23:34,190
Pearson correlation 0.996 with the counted log probabilities.

412
00:23:34,190 --> 00:23:37,517
Raw scores can shift by a different constant

413
00:23:37,517 --> 00:23:40,012
in each row without changing probabilities,

414
00:23:40,012 --> 00:23:42,507
so the comparison uses normalized logs.

415
00:23:42,507 --> 00:23:44,586
Maximum likelihood connects the counting

416
00:23:44,586 --> 00:23:46,665
solution to the optimization objective;

417
00:23:46,665 --> 00:23:49,576
a finite run need not reach it.

418
00:23:49,576 --> 00:23:52,487
The same optimization loop, with richer models,

419
00:23:52,487 --> 00:23:56,897
trains the later systems in this series.

420
00:23:57,164 --> 00:24:01,808
How large a step can plain full-batch gradient descent take here?

421
00:24:01,808 --> 00:24:04,763
Chapter fourteen will derive the local quadratic

422
00:24:04,763 --> 00:24:07,718
stability scale, two over the largest curvature.

423
00:24:07,718 --> 00:24:11,518
It is not a global guarantee as curvature changes.

424
00:24:11,518 --> 00:24:14,051
At the start, every score zero,

425
00:24:14,051 --> 00:24:16,584
the Hessian's largest eigenvalue is 0.0071,

426
00:24:16,584 --> 00:24:19,539
which would allow steps up to 283.

427
00:24:19,539 --> 00:24:22,494
But the counting solution has curvature 0.026,

428
00:24:22,494 --> 00:24:25,027
giving a local scale of 77.

429
00:24:25,027 --> 00:24:27,560
Over the final 500 recorded states,

430
00:24:27,560 --> 00:24:31,782
step 57 has mean loss 3.332 bits and range 0.0001;

431
00:24:31,782 --> 00:24:35,160
step 85 has mean 3.355 and range 0.0097.

432
00:24:35,160 --> 00:24:39,381
Only the first is within our 0.001-bit late-window variation criterion.

433
00:24:39,381 --> 00:24:41,492
That describes this finite window,

434
00:24:41,492 --> 00:24:44,025
not convergence for all future updates.

435
00:24:45,764 --> 00:24:46,671
Chapter nine.

436
00:24:46,671 --> 00:24:49,964
Numbers in a computer.

437
00:24:49,964 --> 00:24:54,461
Every number in this series so far has been a float.

438
00:24:54,461 --> 00:24:58,958
In 1985 one standard, IEEE 754, designed largely by William Kahan,

439
00:24:58,958 --> 00:25:01,002
specified basic operations and rounding.

440
00:25:01,002 --> 00:25:04,273
The same format, operation and rounding mode agree;

441
00:25:04,273 --> 00:25:08,770
changing the order or fusing operations can change a program's answer.

442
00:25:08,770 --> 00:25:13,676
A normal finite float has a sign, an exponent and a mantissa:

443
00:25:13,676 --> 00:25:17,355
one point the mantissa, times two to the exponent.

444
00:25:17,355 --> 00:25:18,991
Exponent bits buy range;

445
00:25:18,991 --> 00:25:20,626
mantissa bits buy precision.

446
00:25:20,626 --> 00:25:22,670
Float32 has eight and twenty-three.

447
00:25:22,670 --> 00:25:25,941
Bfloat16 keeps float32's eight exponent

448
00:25:25,941 --> 00:25:28,803
bits and only seven for the mantissa;

449
00:25:28,803 --> 00:25:31,256
float16 has five and ten;

450
00:25:31,256 --> 00:25:36,979
and the eight-bit formats of 2022 have four and three, or five and two.

451
00:25:36,979 --> 00:25:44,612
The E4M3 finite variant shown here reserves NaN but has no infinities.

452
00:25:44,897 --> 00:25:46,962
The gap between neighbouring floats,

453
00:25:46,962 --> 00:25:51,503
the unit in the last place, grows with the number itself:

454
00:25:51,503 --> 00:25:56,458
a staircase, one step per power of two in the normal range.

455
00:25:56,458 --> 00:25:58,935
Normal relative precision is about 7.2

456
00:25:58,935 --> 00:26:03,064
decimal digits in float32, 2.4 in bfloat16.

457
00:26:03,064 --> 00:26:05,129
Below the smallest normal number,

458
00:26:05,129 --> 00:26:08,019
subnormals instead have a fixed absolute gap:

459
00:26:08,019 --> 00:26:11,322
two to the minus twenty-four for float16.

460
00:26:11,322 --> 00:26:15,038
Relative error can then grow to one on underflow.

461
00:26:15,038 --> 00:26:17,102
The plot enumerates format values;

462
00:26:17,102 --> 00:26:19,992
some device arithmetic flushes subnormals to zero.

463
00:26:19,992 --> 00:26:22,057
Each format also ends somewhere.

464
00:26:22,057 --> 00:26:26,599
Float16 stops at 65,504, the four-bit-exponent FP8 at 448,

465
00:26:26,599 --> 00:26:30,315
while bfloat16 keeps float32's exponent range.

466
00:26:30,315 --> 00:26:34,856
That is why bfloat16, Google's format for its TPUs,

467
00:26:34,856 --> 00:26:38,985
usually avoids the loss scaling often needed with float16.

468
00:26:38,985 --> 00:26:42,701
Part 17 covers the hardware, and Part 19 how

469
00:26:42,701 --> 00:26:47,092
models are squeezed into fewer bits still.

470
00:26:47,364 --> 00:26:48,661
Rounding has consequences.

471
00:26:48,661 --> 00:26:51,687
In bfloat16, one plus one

472
00:26:51,687 --> 00:26:52,119
two-hundred-and-fifty-sixth

473
00:26:52,119 --> 00:26:54,713
is one, and 257 is 256.

474
00:26:54,713 --> 00:26:58,171
Even 0.1 is not 0.1 in float32.

475
00:26:58,171 --> 00:27:00,333
Add 0.01 ten thousand times;

476
00:27:00,333 --> 00:27:02,494
the answer should be 100.

477
00:27:02,494 --> 00:27:03,791
Float32 gets 100.003.

478
00:27:03,791 --> 00:27:08,114
Float16 stalls at 32, and bfloat16 at 4:

479
00:27:08,114 --> 00:27:10,708
once the sum is big enough,

480
00:27:10,708 --> 00:27:14,598
the gap between floats is more than twice 0.01,

481
00:27:14,598 --> 00:27:18,489
and every addition rounds back to where it was.

482
00:27:18,489 --> 00:27:22,812
In this experiment, keep each input in bfloat16

483
00:27:22,812 --> 00:27:25,406
and accumulate sequentially in float32:

484
00:27:25,406 --> 00:27:28,864
100.10, because 0.01 itself was rounded to 0.010010.

485
00:27:28,864 --> 00:27:32,323
Mixed-precision matrix units use the same wider-accumulation idea;

486
00:27:32,323 --> 00:27:35,984
their reduction order can differ.

487
00:27:36,230 --> 00:27:37,862
Range has consequences too.

488
00:27:37,862 --> 00:27:39,902
The softmax exponentiates its scores,

489
00:27:39,902 --> 00:27:45,206
and e to the power 89 is already too big for float32;

490
00:27:45,206 --> 00:27:48,469
in float16, e to the 11.1 is.

491
00:27:48,469 --> 00:27:52,141
So scores of a thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine

492
00:27:52,141 --> 00:27:54,997
and nine hundred and ninety-eight become infinities,

493
00:27:54,997 --> 00:27:57,853
and the probabilities become not a number.

494
00:27:57,853 --> 00:28:01,524
The fix is to subtract the largest score first.

495
00:28:01,524 --> 00:28:05,604
The log of a sum of exponentials is the maximum,

496
00:28:05,604 --> 00:28:11,315
plus the log of a sum in which every exponent is at most zero.

497
00:28:11,315 --> 00:28:14,171
For finite scores, no shifted exponential overflows,

498
00:28:14,171 --> 00:28:19,475
and the largest term is exactly one, so the sum can't vanish either.

499
00:28:19,475 --> 00:28:22,738
Very small terms may underflow harmlessly to zero.

500
00:28:22,738 --> 00:28:26,002
The same three scores now give clean probabilities,

501
00:28:26,002 --> 00:28:30,490
and our library's log-softmax does exactly this, in its first line.

502
00:28:32,230 --> 00:28:33,185
Chapter ten.

503
00:28:33,185 --> 00:28:37,073
Cancellation, and the gradient check.

504
00:28:37,097 --> 00:28:42,884
One more way numbers go wrong, and it explains how to check a gradient.

505
00:28:42,884 --> 00:28:46,192
Take one minus cos x, over x squared:

506
00:28:46,192 --> 00:28:49,912
for small x it is very nearly one half.

507
00:28:49,912 --> 00:28:53,633
Computed as written, in float32, the error explodes

508
00:28:53,633 --> 00:28:58,594
as x shrinks, and at x of ten to the minus four,

509
00:28:58,594 --> 00:29:03,141
cos x rounds to exactly one, and the answer is zero.

510
00:29:03,141 --> 00:29:04,381
This is cancellation:

511
00:29:04,381 --> 00:29:06,862
subtracting two nearly equal numbers keeps

512
00:29:06,862 --> 00:29:09,756
their rounding errors but loses their size,

513
00:29:09,756 --> 00:29:12,649
so the relative error grows like one

514
00:29:12,649 --> 00:29:15,130
over x squared, just as predicted.

515
00:29:15,130 --> 00:29:16,783
Float64 only delays it.

516
00:29:16,783 --> 00:29:19,677
Rewritten without the subtraction, the same formula

517
00:29:19,677 --> 00:29:22,571
avoids that cancellation over this sampled range.

518
00:29:22,571 --> 00:29:29,032
Errors below the display floor, including zero, are drawn at the floor.

519
00:29:29,297 --> 00:29:32,521
A gradient check is a cancellation on purpose.

520
00:29:32,521 --> 00:29:36,955
Expand f a step either side of x, by Taylor's theorem.

521
00:29:36,955 --> 00:29:39,373
Subtract and divide by two h:

522
00:29:39,373 --> 00:29:42,598
the even terms cancel, leaving the derivative plus

523
00:29:42,598 --> 00:29:45,822
a truncation error that shrinks like h squared.

524
00:29:45,822 --> 00:29:50,659
But each value of f was rounded, by about u times f,

525
00:29:50,659 --> 00:29:53,884
u the unit roundoff, and the subtraction keeps

526
00:29:53,884 --> 00:29:57,511
those errors while dividing by a tiny 2 h:

527
00:29:57,511 --> 00:30:01,139
a rounding error that grows like one over h.

528
00:30:01,139 --> 00:30:04,766
Add the two, and set the derivative to zero:

529
00:30:04,766 --> 00:30:07,991
this local model predicts a step near the

530
00:30:07,991 --> 00:30:11,618
cube root of three u times the absolute value

531
00:30:11,618 --> 00:30:14,440
of f over the absolute third derivative,

532
00:30:14,440 --> 00:30:16,455
when that derivative is nonzero.

533
00:30:16,455 --> 00:30:19,680
Too large a step and Taylor's error wins;

534
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:23,195
too small and rounding does.

535
00:30:23,430 --> 00:30:26,705
Here it is measured, on chapter seven's network,

536
00:30:26,705 --> 00:30:30,389
along the same eight random directions in both precisions.

537
00:30:30,389 --> 00:30:33,255
The solid curves are pointwise median errors,

538
00:30:33,255 --> 00:30:36,121
and the shaded bands show the range.

539
00:30:36,121 --> 00:30:38,577
First inspect float64 for the competition

540
00:30:38,577 --> 00:30:40,215
between truncation and rounding.

541
00:30:40,215 --> 00:30:42,671
Its fitted right-hand slope is 2.00.

542
00:30:42,671 --> 00:30:44,308
Now compare float32.

543
00:30:44,308 --> 00:30:48,811
Each dot marks the minimum of the median curve actually drawn;

544
00:30:48,811 --> 00:30:52,905
it is not the median of the eight best steps.

545
00:30:52,905 --> 00:30:55,362
Both plotted median-curve optima are within

546
00:30:55,362 --> 00:30:58,637
a factor of three of the predicted steps.

547
00:30:58,637 --> 00:31:01,093
The smallest median errors are 6.47e-13

548
00:31:01,093 --> 00:31:03,958
in float64 and 2.07e-06 in float32.

549
00:31:03,958 --> 00:31:07,643
For comparison, At chapter seven's step of a millionth,

550
00:31:07,643 --> 00:31:10,099
the float64 median error is 5.87e-11.

551
00:31:10,099 --> 00:31:13,374
The local error model guides the step choice;

552
00:31:13,374 --> 00:31:15,421
the measured curve checks it.

553
00:31:15,421 --> 00:31:17,877
Values outside the displayed error range

554
00:31:17,877 --> 00:31:21,152
are shown at its stated floor or ceiling.

555
00:31:22,896 --> 00:31:23,970
Chapter eleven.

556
00:31:23,970 --> 00:31:26,007
Memory.

557
00:31:26,029 --> 00:31:29,707
Reverse mode has a price, and it is memory.

558
00:31:29,707 --> 00:31:33,793
On the ordinary forward pass, every layer's output is kept,

559
00:31:33,793 --> 00:31:37,470
because on the way back each step needs it:

560
00:31:37,470 --> 00:31:40,331
a layer's Jacobian depends on its input.

561
00:31:40,331 --> 00:31:45,642
Only as the backward pass reaches a layer can its activations be freed.

562
00:31:45,642 --> 00:31:48,503
So the memory grows with the depth,

563
00:31:48,503 --> 00:31:51,772
and with the batch and the width too;

564
00:31:51,772 --> 00:31:53,815
training large models, activations can

565
00:31:53,815 --> 00:31:56,675
become a major share of GPU memory,

566
00:31:56,675 --> 00:31:58,718
alongside parameters and optimizer state,

567
00:31:58,718 --> 00:32:02,261
which is Part 18's problem.

568
00:32:02,496 --> 00:32:06,119
The way out is to trade memory for time.

569
00:32:06,119 --> 00:32:08,937
Keep only every k-th output, a checkpoint;

570
00:32:08,937 --> 00:32:11,755
when the backward pass reaches a segment,

571
00:32:11,755 --> 00:32:14,976
run that segment forward again from its checkpoint,

572
00:32:14,976 --> 00:32:18,599
and keep its k outputs just while they're needed.

573
00:32:18,599 --> 00:32:23,430
The memory is n over k checkpoints plus one segment of k.

574
00:32:23,430 --> 00:32:26,248
It's smallest where its slope is zero,

575
00:32:26,248 --> 00:32:29,871
at k equal to the square root of n,

576
00:32:29,871 --> 00:32:33,495
where it is two root n instead of n.

577
00:32:33,495 --> 00:32:34,300
The cost:

578
00:32:34,300 --> 00:32:38,326
every segment is recomputed once, about one extra forward pass.

579
00:32:38,326 --> 00:32:42,754
Tianqi Chen and colleagues brought this to deep networks in 2016;

580
00:32:42,754 --> 00:32:46,780
Andreas Griewank had shown in 1992 that recursive checkpoint schedules

581
00:32:46,780 --> 00:32:50,000
can use logarithmic storage and logarithmic recomputation overhead,

582
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:52,818
relative to the original linear evaluation cost:

583
00:32:52,818 --> 00:32:56,844
total work of order n log n in that model.

584
00:32:56,844 --> 00:32:59,662
Today it is one line in PyTorch,

585
00:32:59,662 --> 00:33:02,078
and FlashAttention uses the same trade,

586
00:33:02,078 --> 00:33:04,493
recomputing attention's weights in the backward

587
00:33:04,493 --> 00:33:09,616
pass instead of storing them, as Part 19 shows.

588
00:33:09,896 --> 00:33:14,976
Counted in an ideal activation-liveness model, on a chain of 256 layers.

589
00:33:14,976 --> 00:33:20,055
The most activations held at once follows n over k plus k:

590
00:33:20,055 --> 00:33:24,288
257 if you keep everything, and 32 at the minimum.

591
00:33:24,288 --> 00:33:28,098
The minimizing intervals span k of 13 to 20.

592
00:33:28,098 --> 00:33:31,484
The square-root reference, 16, lies in that span.

593
00:33:31,484 --> 00:33:33,600
The price is in evaluations:

594
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:36,987
256 layers run forward if everything is kept,

595
00:33:36,987 --> 00:33:40,373
496 with checkpoints, and 33,152 if nothing is

596
00:33:40,373 --> 00:33:44,183
kept and every input is recomputed from the start.

597
00:33:44,183 --> 00:33:46,299
Across the tested longer-chain candidates,

598
00:33:46,299 --> 00:33:50,955
selected k divided by root n ranges from 1.00 to 1.25.

599
00:33:50,955 --> 00:33:53,495
Timed on a deeper, wider chain,

600
00:33:53,495 --> 00:33:57,305
checkpoints cost 1.14 times the time in this benchmark.

601
00:33:57,305 --> 00:34:00,268
The maximum absolute gradient discrepancy is 0.00e+00.

602
00:34:00,268 --> 00:34:04,077
This counts logical activation lifetimes, not measured physical memory;

603
00:34:04,077 --> 00:34:05,770
parameters, gradients, allocator behavior

604
00:34:05,770 --> 00:34:07,887
and temporary workspace are excluded.

605
00:34:09,629 --> 00:34:10,703
Chapter twelve.

606
00:34:10,703 --> 00:34:12,740
Curvature.

607
00:34:12,762 --> 00:34:15,631
The gradient says which way is downhill.

608
00:34:15,631 --> 00:34:19,320
It doesn't say how the slope itself is changing.

609
00:34:19,320 --> 00:34:21,369
That's the second derivative, curvature.

610
00:34:21,369 --> 00:34:25,877
With many inputs it's a matrix of second derivatives, the Hessian.

611
00:34:25,877 --> 00:34:29,566
For a twice continuously differentiable function it is symmetric,

612
00:34:29,566 --> 00:34:32,845
so, from part three, it has perpendicular eigenvectors,

613
00:34:32,845 --> 00:34:34,894
the directions of pure curvature,

614
00:34:34,894 --> 00:34:39,402
and its eigenvalues say how sharply the surface bends along each.

615
00:34:39,402 --> 00:34:43,091
This quadratic is a bowl when both are positive.

616
00:34:43,091 --> 00:34:47,599
With mixed signs it is a saddle at its stationary origin.

617
00:34:47,599 --> 00:34:53,198
Mixed curvature at an arbitrary point does not establish stationarity.

618
00:34:53,629 --> 00:34:56,092
The Hessian gives a better step.

619
00:34:56,092 --> 00:34:59,376
The first derivative made the best straight line;

620
00:34:59,376 --> 00:35:01,839
with the second, the local quadratic.

621
00:35:01,839 --> 00:35:06,354
When the Hessian is positive definite, this quadratic has a minimum.

622
00:35:06,354 --> 00:35:10,049
Solve for that minimum, where its gradient is zero:

623
00:35:10,049 --> 00:35:13,743
the step is minus H inverse times the gradient.

624
00:35:13,743 --> 00:35:14,975
That's Newton's method.

625
00:35:14,975 --> 00:35:19,080
Newton used the idea in 1669 for roots of equations,

626
00:35:19,080 --> 00:35:22,774
Raphson simplified it in 1690, and Thomas Simpson extended

627
00:35:22,774 --> 00:35:28,379
it in 1740 to several variables and to finding minima.

628
00:35:28,629 --> 00:35:29,453
A test:

629
00:35:29,453 --> 00:35:32,335
logistic regression, a classifier Part 6 builds,

630
00:35:32,335 --> 00:35:33,982
with two correlated features.

631
00:35:33,982 --> 00:35:37,688
The curvature ratio at the reference solution is 187.

632
00:35:37,688 --> 00:35:41,393
Gradient descent uses the conservative step one over L,

633
00:35:41,393 --> 00:35:43,452
where L globally bounds curvature.

634
00:35:43,452 --> 00:35:46,746
Gradient descent first meets its objective gap to

635
00:35:46,746 --> 00:35:49,628
the numerical reference threshold at step 2362.

636
00:35:49,628 --> 00:35:53,746
Newton first meets its gradient norm threshold at step 4.

637
00:35:53,746 --> 00:35:58,275
These are iteration counts, not equal amounts of work per step.

638
00:35:58,275 --> 00:36:01,157
Correct-digit estimates are 2.9, 5.7, and 11.0.

639
00:36:01,157 --> 00:36:04,040
The recorded counts increase in these steps.

640
00:36:04,040 --> 00:36:06,922
Local quadratic convergence requires a sufficiently close

641
00:36:06,922 --> 00:36:10,628
starting point and a nonsingular Hessian with suitable smoothness;

642
00:36:10,628 --> 00:36:13,922
three digit counts alone do not certify it.

643
00:36:13,922 --> 00:36:16,392
The catch is the Hessian itself:

644
00:36:16,392 --> 00:36:19,686
each step solves a linear system with it,

645
00:36:19,686 --> 00:36:24,627
and for a billion parameters it would have a billion squared entries.

646
00:36:24,627 --> 00:36:28,333
The next chapter uses curvature without ever building it.

647
00:36:30,095 --> 00:36:31,003
Chapter thirteen.

648
00:36:31,003 --> 00:36:34,295
Curvature without the Hessian.

649
00:36:34,295 --> 00:36:38,322
A Hessian with a billion rows is out of reach,

650
00:36:38,322 --> 00:36:41,543
but its product with one vector is not.

651
00:36:41,543 --> 00:36:46,777
H times v is how the gradient changes as you move along v.

652
00:36:46,777 --> 00:36:50,401
Since v is fixed, that's the gradient of a

653
00:36:50,401 --> 00:36:53,219
single number, the gradient dotted with v.

654
00:36:53,219 --> 00:36:58,051
And a gradient of one number is what reverse mode does cheaply:

655
00:36:58,051 --> 00:37:02,479
run it once more, through the computation of the gradient itself,

656
00:37:02,479 --> 00:37:08,921
and you get H v exactly, for a small multiple of the cost of a gradient.

657
00:37:08,921 --> 00:37:13,240
Barak Pearlmutter published the trick in 1994.

658
00:37:13,495 --> 00:37:18,055
Check it on the stored final parameters of a small network.

659
00:37:18,055 --> 00:37:22,200
Against the full Hessian, the maximum product discrepancy is 1.08e-17.

660
00:37:22,200 --> 00:37:26,759
In this benchmark a product takes 2.3 times a gradient's time.

661
00:37:26,759 --> 00:37:30,075
The best recorded finite-difference relative error is 2.30e-09.

662
00:37:30,075 --> 00:37:32,976
Repeated products and normalization are power iteration.

663
00:37:32,976 --> 00:37:37,121
With a strictly dominant eigenvalue magnitude and a nonzero initial

664
00:37:37,121 --> 00:37:40,437
projection on its eigenspace, they approach that eigenspace.

665
00:37:40,437 --> 00:37:42,510
The eigenvalue may be negative:

666
00:37:42,510 --> 00:37:45,411
largest magnitude is not largest algebraic value.

667
00:37:45,411 --> 00:37:47,898
For this stored spectrum the signed

668
00:37:47,898 --> 00:37:49,556
dominant-magnitude target is 0.00655;

669
00:37:49,556 --> 00:37:52,043
the final relative error is 3.39e-14.

670
00:37:52,043 --> 00:37:57,273
Display floors and ceilings are stated below the plots.

671
00:37:57,562 --> 00:38:00,384
Now watch a landscape change as a

672
00:38:00,384 --> 00:38:02,399
network learns three interleaved spirals.

673
00:38:02,399 --> 00:38:04,415
This network has 371 parameters,

674
00:38:04,415 --> 00:38:08,043
with its full Hessian computed at 6 stored checkpoints.

675
00:38:08,043 --> 00:38:10,865
Accuracy is on the 300 training examples.

676
00:38:10,865 --> 00:38:14,896
The initial Hessian has both positive and negative curvature directions.

677
00:38:14,896 --> 00:38:18,524
That alone does not establish a stationary saddle point.

678
00:38:18,524 --> 00:38:22,958
The signed-log plot divides each spectrum by its own absolute spectral

679
00:38:22,958 --> 00:38:27,392
radius, so its shape and absolute size must be read separately.

680
00:38:27,392 --> 00:38:30,617
The count below the stated negative cutoff decreases

681
00:38:30,617 --> 00:38:34,648
from 72 to 0 between the first and final checkpoints.

682
00:38:34,648 --> 00:38:35,454
Final counts:

683
00:38:35,454 --> 00:38:39,485
At the final checkpoint, 4 eigenvalues exceed the chosen positive

684
00:38:39,485 --> 00:38:43,920
cutoff, and 95 percent lie inside the stated relative near-zero cutoff.

685
00:38:43,920 --> 00:38:44,726
Absolute scale:

686
00:38:44,726 --> 00:38:48,354
The absolute spectral radius decreases from 0.955 to 0.00655.

687
00:38:48,354 --> 00:38:52,385
The cutoffs are stated relative to each checkpoint's spectral radius.

688
00:38:52,385 --> 00:38:54,400
High-positive is a selected cutoff,

689
00:38:54,400 --> 00:38:56,819
not a fitted statistical outlier test.

690
00:38:56,819 --> 00:38:58,835
Sagun and colleagues studied concentration

691
00:38:58,835 --> 00:39:01,656
of neural network Hessian spectra in 2016;

692
00:39:01,656 --> 00:39:05,284
this small training example reports its own measured outcome.

693
00:39:07,028 --> 00:39:07,936
Chapter fourteen.

694
00:39:07,936 --> 00:39:11,228
How fast is downhill?

695
00:39:11,228 --> 00:39:14,456
On a quadratic bowl with a positive-definite Hessian,

696
00:39:14,456 --> 00:39:16,876
gradient descent can be solved exactly.

697
00:39:16,876 --> 00:39:20,911
Each step multiplies the position by I minus eta H.

698
00:39:20,911 --> 00:39:24,138
In the Hessian's eigenvectors that matrix is diagonal,

699
00:39:24,138 --> 00:39:26,962
so each coordinate shrinks on its own,

700
00:39:26,962 --> 00:39:29,786
by one minus eta times its eigenvalue.

701
00:39:29,786 --> 00:39:35,031
Every factor must be less than one in size, so the step must

702
00:39:35,031 --> 00:39:39,065
be positive and smaller than two over the largest curvature.

703
00:39:39,065 --> 00:39:41,889
And the slowest coordinate sets the pace.

704
00:39:41,889 --> 00:39:44,713
The best step balances the two extremes,

705
00:39:44,713 --> 00:39:49,958
and the rate is kappa minus one over kappa plus one, where kappa,

706
00:39:49,958 --> 00:39:53,992
the condition number, is the largest curvature over the smallest.

707
00:39:53,992 --> 00:39:56,412
This factor bounds the position-error norm;

708
00:39:56,412 --> 00:40:01,543
the quadratic objective shrinks by at most its square.

709
00:40:01,828 --> 00:40:05,002
Here is a valley with kappa of twenty-five:

710
00:40:05,002 --> 00:40:08,572
it bends twenty-five times more sharply across than along.

711
00:40:08,572 --> 00:40:12,539
The steepest way down points mostly at the opposite wall,

712
00:40:12,539 --> 00:40:17,696
so the steps zigzag from wall to wall and crawl along the floor.

713
00:40:17,696 --> 00:40:22,456
For the objective to fall below one percent of its initial value,

714
00:40:22,456 --> 00:40:25,233
the threshold was reached after 26 updates.

715
00:40:25,233 --> 00:40:28,010
Momentum carries speed from step to step.

716
00:40:28,010 --> 00:40:30,390
Momentum has a smaller final position-error

717
00:40:30,390 --> 00:40:32,770
norm over this equal update budget.

718
00:40:32,770 --> 00:40:34,753
Part 11 derives the method.

719
00:40:34,753 --> 00:40:41,014
Newton's step undoes the curvature and lands at the bottom at once.

720
00:40:41,295 --> 00:40:45,428
Measured on bowls from kappa of one to a thousand:

721
00:40:45,428 --> 00:40:48,321
the rates, in orange, match the predicted

722
00:40:48,321 --> 00:40:50,388
norm-error factors within one millionth.

723
00:40:50,388 --> 00:40:53,694
The grey curve is the large-kappa norm-error estimate.

724
00:40:53,694 --> 00:40:57,827
At kappa 1,000, the threshold was reached after 6,908 updates.

725
00:40:57,827 --> 00:41:01,547
Triangles mark capped or unresolved runs, not converged counts.

726
00:41:01,547 --> 00:41:05,680
The theoretical stability boundary is two over the largest curvature.

727
00:41:05,680 --> 00:41:09,399
The first sampled step size whose norm after one

728
00:41:09,399 --> 00:41:12,706
hundred updates exceeds its initial norm is 0.082.

729
00:41:12,706 --> 00:41:16,012
Real losses are not quadratics, but this is

730
00:41:16,012 --> 00:41:18,492
the calculus behind every learning-rate rule,

731
00:41:18,492 --> 00:41:23,712
and Part 11 builds the faster methods on it.

732
00:41:23,962 --> 00:41:25,973
A saddle can delay descent.

733
00:41:25,973 --> 00:41:29,192
Here's a surface with a saddle at the

734
00:41:29,192 --> 00:41:32,410
centre and a minimum above and below it.

735
00:41:32,410 --> 00:41:35,629
Start almost on the line through the saddle,

736
00:41:35,629 --> 00:41:38,445
a tiny distance y nought off it.

737
00:41:38,445 --> 00:41:41,261
The walk slides in towards the saddle,

738
00:41:41,261 --> 00:41:44,078
where the gradient nearly vanishes, and waits,

739
00:41:44,078 --> 00:41:48,101
until the tiny offset has grown enough to fall off.

740
00:41:48,101 --> 00:41:52,928
Near the saddle the offset grows by one plus eta each step,

741
00:41:52,928 --> 00:41:57,354
so the local prediction is logarithmic in one over y nought.

742
00:41:57,354 --> 00:42:01,779
We fit only runs that reached absolute y above one half;

743
00:42:01,779 --> 00:42:06,607
the measured slope is within ten percent of the local linear prediction.

744
00:42:06,607 --> 00:42:08,619
Capped runs are marked separately.

745
00:42:08,619 --> 00:42:12,240
Start exactly on the line and it never leaves:

746
00:42:12,240 --> 00:42:16,665
the gradient falls to 3 times ten to the minus 14,

747
00:42:16,665 --> 00:42:19,884
at a point that is not a minimum.

748
00:42:19,884 --> 00:42:23,907
Only the Hessian, with its negative eigenvalue, tells them apart.

749
00:42:23,907 --> 00:42:28,332
In practice the noise of stochastic gradients kicks a network off;

750
00:42:28,332 --> 00:42:30,746
Yann Dauphin and colleagues argued in

751
00:42:30,746 --> 00:42:33,160
2014 that in many dimensions saddles,

752
00:42:33,160 --> 00:42:36,378
more than bad minima, are what slow training.

753
00:42:38,161 --> 00:42:39,102
Chapter fifteen.

754
00:42:39,102 --> 00:42:42,013
Optimizing under constraints.

755
00:42:42,028 --> 00:42:44,090
Often the numbers aren't free:

756
00:42:44,090 --> 00:42:48,215
probabilities must add to one, a budget must be kept.

757
00:42:48,215 --> 00:42:50,690
Here, minimize f, whose contours are

758
00:42:50,690 --> 00:42:53,577
these ellipses, but only on the circle.

759
00:42:53,577 --> 00:42:56,876
Walk along the circle and watch two arrows:

760
00:42:56,876 --> 00:43:01,001
the gradient of f, and the gradient of the constraint,

761
00:43:01,001 --> 00:43:03,888
which points straight out of the circle.

762
00:43:03,888 --> 00:43:08,425
Wherever the gradient of f has a part along the circle,

763
00:43:08,425 --> 00:43:12,137
you can slide against that part and go lower.

764
00:43:12,137 --> 00:43:15,849
So at the constrained minimum that part is zero:

765
00:43:15,849 --> 00:43:17,911
the two gradients are parallel.

766
00:43:17,911 --> 00:43:20,386
Joseph-Louis Lagrange turned that picture into

767
00:43:20,386 --> 00:43:25,186
a method in 1788, in his Mécanique analytique.

768
00:43:25,428 --> 00:43:26,658
Write it down:

769
00:43:26,658 --> 00:43:31,169
at the constrained minimum, the gradient of f is a multiple

770
00:43:31,169 --> 00:43:34,450
of the constraint's gradient, minus lambda times it.

771
00:43:34,450 --> 00:43:38,141
Lagrange's device folds the constraint into a single function,

772
00:43:38,141 --> 00:43:41,012
the Lagrangian, f plus lambda times g.

773
00:43:41,012 --> 00:43:44,702
Stationarity in x and lambda gives necessary conditions for

774
00:43:44,702 --> 00:43:48,803
a regular constrained optimum, not a guarantee of a minimum.

775
00:43:48,803 --> 00:43:52,084
The derivative in lambda gives back the constraint.

776
00:43:52,084 --> 00:43:55,775
At the numerical circle candidate, the multiplier is 0.626.

777
00:43:55,775 --> 00:43:57,415
The optimizer reports success;

778
00:43:57,415 --> 00:44:01,106
constraint and stationarity residuals are at most one millionth.

779
00:44:01,106 --> 00:44:04,797
At a regular optimum it is a signed price:

780
00:44:04,797 --> 00:44:10,808
how the optimal value changes as the circle's squared radius changes.

781
00:44:11,095 --> 00:44:12,769
A famous constrained problem:

782
00:44:12,769 --> 00:44:16,537
a die whose average roll is 4.5, not 3.5.

783
00:44:16,537 --> 00:44:19,467
Which probabilities assume the least beyond that?

784
00:44:19,467 --> 00:44:21,979
The ones with the most entropy.

785
00:44:21,979 --> 00:44:24,490
Set the Lagrangian's derivative to zero,

786
00:44:24,490 --> 00:44:29,514
with one multiplier for each constraint, and the answer is a softmax:

787
00:44:29,514 --> 00:44:34,537
each face's probability is e to the beta times the face, normalized.

788
00:44:34,537 --> 00:44:36,212
Computed, beta is 0.371.

789
00:44:36,212 --> 00:44:38,723
The comparison solver reports success and

790
00:44:38,723 --> 00:44:41,235
agrees within the stated probability tolerance.

791
00:44:41,235 --> 00:44:45,421
Edwin Jaynes made this a principle of inference in 1957.

792
00:44:45,421 --> 00:44:47,933
It's where the softmax comes from:

793
00:44:47,933 --> 00:44:52,119
it is the maximum-entropy distribution with a fixed average score.

794
00:44:52,119 --> 00:44:55,049
In this convention beta is inverse temperature,

795
00:44:55,049 --> 00:45:00,317
one over T, and it is the score-constraint multiplier.

796
00:45:00,561 --> 00:45:04,976
The same algebra gives reinforcement learning one of its closed forms.

797
00:45:04,976 --> 00:45:08,186
Maximize the expected reward, minus a positive beta

798
00:45:08,186 --> 00:45:12,199
times the KL divergence from a positive reference policy.

799
00:45:12,199 --> 00:45:17,416
Here beta names the KL penalty, so it plays the temperature role,

800
00:45:17,416 --> 00:45:20,225
unlike the inverse-temperature beta for the die.

801
00:45:20,225 --> 00:45:23,837
With the constraint that the probabilities add to one,

802
00:45:23,837 --> 00:45:26,245
the best policy is the reference,

803
00:45:26,245 --> 00:45:29,455
reweighted by e to the reward over beta.

804
00:45:29,455 --> 00:45:31,060
Here for six actions.

805
00:45:31,060 --> 00:45:33,468
The comparison solver reports success and

806
00:45:33,468 --> 00:45:35,876
agrees within the stated probability tolerance.

807
00:45:35,876 --> 00:45:39,487
Part 27's direct preference optimization starts from this formula.

808
00:45:41,227 --> 00:45:42,268
Chapter sixteen.

809
00:45:42,268 --> 00:45:45,329
Inequalities, and duality.

810
00:45:45,361 --> 00:45:46,997
Most constraints are inequalities:

811
00:45:46,997 --> 00:45:51,907
a probability can't be negative, a margin must be at least one.

812
00:45:51,907 --> 00:45:56,408
William Karush worked out the conditions in his 1939 master's thesis;

813
00:45:56,408 --> 00:46:00,500
Harold Kuhn and Albert Tucker found them again in 1951.

814
00:46:00,500 --> 00:46:05,001
Under a constraint qualification, at a minimum there are multipliers mu,

815
00:46:05,001 --> 00:46:09,092
one per constraint, with the gradient of the Lagrangian zero,

816
00:46:09,092 --> 00:46:11,547
every constraint met, every multiplier non-negative,

817
00:46:11,547 --> 00:46:15,230
and each multiplier times its constraint equal to zero:

818
00:46:15,230 --> 00:46:16,048
complementary slackness.

819
00:46:16,048 --> 00:46:20,140
Either the minimum is inside and the wall is idle,

820
00:46:20,140 --> 00:46:24,231
its multiplier zero, or the minimum is on the wall.

821
00:46:24,231 --> 00:46:27,095
Its multiplier may be positive or zero;

822
00:46:27,095 --> 00:46:29,960
in this right-hand example it is positive

823
00:46:29,960 --> 00:46:34,324
and measures how hard the wall pushes.

824
00:46:34,561 --> 00:46:37,496
The multipliers lead to a second problem.

825
00:46:37,496 --> 00:46:42,527
Fix them, and ask how low the Lagrangian can go over x:

826
00:46:42,527 --> 00:46:45,462
that's the dual function, q of mu.

827
00:46:45,462 --> 00:46:50,074
For any feasible x the added terms are at most zero,

828
00:46:50,074 --> 00:46:56,364
so the dual function is a lower bound on f, everywhere it can be met.

829
00:46:56,364 --> 00:47:02,233
The best lower bound, d star, is at most the true minimum, p star:

830
00:47:02,233 --> 00:47:04,749
weak duality, true for every problem.

831
00:47:04,749 --> 00:47:09,781
For convex problems with a strictly feasible point, the two are equal:

832
00:47:09,781 --> 00:47:10,619
strong duality.

833
00:47:10,619 --> 00:47:14,635
Both give the same optimal value.

834
00:47:14,894 --> 00:47:16,180
Two small examples.

835
00:47:16,180 --> 00:47:19,608
Minimize x squared, keeping x at least one.

836
00:47:19,608 --> 00:47:23,036
Its dual function is a hump that rises

837
00:47:23,036 --> 00:47:26,035
to touch the true minimum, one, exactly:

838
00:47:26,035 --> 00:47:26,892
no gap.

839
00:47:26,892 --> 00:47:32,035
Now a double well, the same kind of constraint, and no convexity.

840
00:47:32,035 --> 00:47:36,748
On finite grids, the estimated primal value is -1.07 and the

841
00:47:36,748 --> 00:47:41,033
best estimated dual value is -1.75, a difference of 0.68.

842
00:47:41,033 --> 00:47:45,318
These are grid estimates, not a certified continuous dual bound.

843
00:47:45,318 --> 00:47:47,889
The exact weak-duality theorem still holds;

844
00:47:47,889 --> 00:47:53,246
this numerical picture illustrates how a gap can arise.

845
00:47:53,494 --> 00:47:55,094
Duality's most famous use:

846
00:47:55,094 --> 00:47:57,894
the support vector machine of Part 6.

847
00:47:57,894 --> 00:48:00,693
Find the widest street between two classes:

848
00:48:00,693 --> 00:48:03,893
the smallest w that puts every point at

849
00:48:03,893 --> 00:48:07,093
least one unit of margin from the line.

850
00:48:07,093 --> 00:48:09,892
Its dual has one multiplier per point,

851
00:48:09,892 --> 00:48:13,092
and depends on the points only through their

852
00:48:13,092 --> 00:48:16,292
dot products, which is what lets kernels in.

853
00:48:16,292 --> 00:48:19,091
Minimizing over the free intercept also requires

854
00:48:19,091 --> 00:48:21,891
the label-weighted multipliers to sum to zero.

855
00:48:21,891 --> 00:48:25,891
We attempted separate primal and dual solves on 20 points.

856
00:48:25,891 --> 00:48:29,090
Both optimizers report success and all listed feasibility,

857
00:48:29,090 --> 00:48:32,290
stationarity, slackness and gap checks pass their tolerances.

858
00:48:32,290 --> 00:48:36,289
The candidate objective values are displayed with six decimal places.

859
00:48:36,289 --> 00:48:39,089
We ring the points whose 3 multipliers

860
00:48:39,089 --> 00:48:41,489
exceed the stated numerical zero threshold;

861
00:48:41,489 --> 00:48:45,488
3 points have margins within the stated tolerance of one.

862
00:48:45,488 --> 00:48:50,288
At an optimum, a positive multiplier forces a point onto a margin,

863
00:48:50,288 --> 00:48:55,087
but a margin point can have a zero multiplier in degenerate data.

864
00:48:55,087 --> 00:48:57,487
Bernhard Boser, Isabelle Guyon and Vladimir

865
00:48:57,487 --> 00:49:01,087
Vapnik built the method on this dual in 1992.

866
00:49:02,827 --> 00:49:03,735
Chapter seventeen.

867
00:49:03,735 --> 00:49:07,027
Differentiating through an equation.

868
00:49:07,027 --> 00:49:10,282
Some layers aren't a fixed list of steps.

869
00:49:10,282 --> 00:49:13,130
A deep equilibrium layer feeds its output

870
00:49:13,130 --> 00:49:15,572
back into itself until nothing changes:

871
00:49:15,572 --> 00:49:18,827
its output is the fixed point z star,

872
00:49:18,827 --> 00:49:23,302
which satisfies z star equals f of z star and theta.

873
00:49:23,302 --> 00:49:25,743
How do you backpropagate through that?

874
00:49:25,743 --> 00:49:28,185
Differentiate both sides of the equation.

875
00:49:28,185 --> 00:49:29,405
Collect the terms:

876
00:49:29,405 --> 00:49:34,288
the derivative of the solution is the inverse of I minus J,

877
00:49:34,288 --> 00:49:38,356
times the direct derivative, as long as that inverse exists,

878
00:49:38,356 --> 00:49:40,797
which is the implicit function theorem.

879
00:49:40,797 --> 00:49:44,459
For a loss, run it as a vector-Jacobian product:

880
00:49:44,459 --> 00:49:47,307
one linear solve at the fixed point,

881
00:49:47,307 --> 00:49:50,562
however many iterations it took to get there.

882
00:49:50,562 --> 00:49:54,224
No history of iteration states is needed, but parameters,

883
00:49:54,224 --> 00:49:57,886
local derivatives and the linear solver still take memory.

884
00:49:57,886 --> 00:50:04,268
If the loss also depends directly on theta, add that direct derivative.

885
00:50:04,561 --> 00:50:08,232
Check it on a layer with an 8-dimensional state;

886
00:50:08,232 --> 00:50:11,902
the iteration met its residual tolerance after 29 updates.

887
00:50:11,902 --> 00:50:13,942
The displayed residual and contraction

888
00:50:13,942 --> 00:50:15,981
bound describe this iteration solver;

889
00:50:15,981 --> 00:50:20,060
invertibility of I minus J is the separate differentiation condition.

890
00:50:20,060 --> 00:50:22,507
The usual alternative is to unroll:

891
00:50:22,507 --> 00:50:27,402
run K iterations and backpropagate through all of them with our library.

892
00:50:27,402 --> 00:50:31,888
As K grows, that gradient is compared with the implicit candidate.

893
00:50:31,888 --> 00:50:34,335
The fitted per-step factor is 0.460,

894
00:50:34,335 --> 00:50:38,414
within 0.15 of the spectral radius in this measured fit.

895
00:50:38,414 --> 00:50:41,269
For the final comparisons, the smallest final

896
00:50:41,269 --> 00:50:42,901
gradient-comparison difference is 8.75824e-13.

897
00:50:42,901 --> 00:50:46,571
This is not a certified bound on fixed-point accuracy.

898
00:50:46,571 --> 00:50:48,203
Unrolling stores every iteration;

899
00:50:48,203 --> 00:50:50,650
implicit differentiation avoids that history while

900
00:50:50,650 --> 00:50:55,005
retaining the equilibrium state and linear-solver workspace.

901
00:50:55,294 --> 00:50:56,508
And it trains.

902
00:50:56,508 --> 00:51:00,556
A deep equilibrium model with a 12-dimensional state and 181

903
00:51:00,556 --> 00:51:03,795
trainable scalars is fitted using implicit candidate gradients,

904
00:51:03,795 --> 00:51:08,247
with its feedback matrix kept at spectral norm at most 0.9.

905
00:51:08,247 --> 00:51:10,676
The stored fits replay the run;

906
00:51:10,676 --> 00:51:15,534
the final recorded loss is lower than the initial loss, and mean

907
00:51:15,534 --> 00:51:19,582
iteration work increases from 10 updates initially to 28 finally.

908
00:51:19,582 --> 00:51:22,010
Of 38448 training solves, 38448 converged,

909
00:51:22,010 --> 00:51:25,249
0 hit the cap and 0 were non-finite.

910
00:51:25,249 --> 00:51:28,487
Capped solves are not counted as converged equilibria.

911
00:51:28,487 --> 00:51:31,320
For the final comparison input, the iteration

912
00:51:31,320 --> 00:51:34,154
met its residual tolerance after 22 updates.

913
00:51:34,154 --> 00:51:36,583
Shaojie Bai, Zico Kolter and Vladlen

914
00:51:36,583 --> 00:51:39,012
Koltun proposed such models in 2019.

915
00:51:39,012 --> 00:51:42,250
The same trick differentiates through an inner optimization,

916
00:51:42,250 --> 00:51:46,298
in meta-learning, or through a solver used as a layer:

917
00:51:46,298 --> 00:51:51,155
wherever the answer is defined by an equation rather than a recipe.

918
00:51:52,893 --> 00:51:54,132
Let's take stock.

919
00:51:54,132 --> 00:51:57,021
A derivative is the best straight line,

920
00:51:57,021 --> 00:52:00,737
the one whose error shrinks faster than the step;

921
00:52:00,737 --> 00:52:04,039
a gradient, the steepest direction, by Cauchy–Schwarz;

922
00:52:04,039 --> 00:52:07,341
a Jacobian, the best matrix, and real layers'

923
00:52:07,341 --> 00:52:10,231
Jacobians need not be formed, only multiplied.

924
00:52:10,231 --> 00:52:14,359
For the full Jacobian, forward mode pays per input and

925
00:52:14,359 --> 00:52:17,661
reverse mode per output, so training runs backwards:

926
00:52:17,661 --> 00:52:19,725
backpropagation, whose weight gradients are

927
00:52:19,725 --> 00:52:23,440
outer products of an error signal and an input.

928
00:52:23,440 --> 00:52:27,155
We built it, checked it and trained with it.

929
00:52:27,155 --> 00:52:30,045
Floats round and cancel, so softmaxes subtract

930
00:52:30,045 --> 00:52:33,347
their maximum and gradient checks pick their step.

931
00:52:33,347 --> 00:52:35,411
Checkpoints trade memory for recomputation

932
00:52:35,411 --> 00:52:37,888
work in an ideal activation-memory model.

933
00:52:37,888 --> 00:52:40,365
Curvature sets the pace of descent,

934
00:52:40,365 --> 00:52:42,842
and Hessian-vector products reach it cheaply.

935
00:52:42,842 --> 00:52:44,493
Our measured spectrum distinguishes

936
00:52:44,493 --> 00:52:46,557
directions using stated relative cutoffs.

937
00:52:46,557 --> 00:52:49,859
Constraints bring multipliers, KKT and a dual problem:

938
00:52:49,859 --> 00:52:51,923
positive multipliers imply active constraints,

939
00:52:51,923 --> 00:52:56,051
and a free SVM intercept adds a label-weighted zero-sum constraint.

940
00:52:56,051 --> 00:52:58,528
An equation's solution can be differentiated

941
00:52:58,528 --> 00:53:02,505
when its local implicit-function conditions hold.

942
00:53:02,760 --> 00:53:05,957
This is the engine of everything that follows.

943
00:53:05,957 --> 00:53:10,352
Our library trains the series' first neural networks in Part 9;

944
00:53:10,352 --> 00:53:12,750
Jacobians multiplied through depth decide whether

945
00:53:12,750 --> 00:53:16,346
deep networks can learn, in Parts 9 and 10;

946
00:53:16,346 --> 00:53:19,543
curvature, saddles and better optimizers fill Part 11;

947
00:53:19,543 --> 00:53:23,139
duality builds the support vector machine in Part 6,

948
00:53:23,139 --> 00:53:26,336
and the closed form of chapter fifteen becomes

949
00:53:26,336 --> 00:53:28,734
direct preference optimization in Part 27;

950
00:53:28,734 --> 00:53:35,029
and number formats and memory come back in Parts 17 to 19.

